\section{ergm and infernet}{

ergm names an attribute with a string, and infernet with a bare name.\tabular{ll}{
   infernet \tab ergm \cr
   \code{ego(x)} \tab \code{nodeocov("x")}, \code{nodeofactor("x")}; \code{b1cov("x")} in a two-mode network; \code{nodecov("x")} in an undirected one \cr
   \code{alter(x)} \tab \code{nodeicov("x")}, \code{nodeifactor("x")}; \code{b2cov("x")} in a two-mode network \cr
   \code{same(x)} \tab \code{nodematch("x")} \cr
   \code{match(x)} \tab \code{nodematch("x", diff = TRUE)} \cr
   \code{mix(x)} \tab \code{nodemix("x")} \cr
   \code{diff(x)} \tab \code{absdiff("x")} \cr
   \code{diff(x, transformer_fn = identity)} \tab \code{diff("x")} \cr
   a layer, or \code{tie(layer)} \tab \code{edgecov()} \cr
   \code{recip} \tab \code{mutual} \cr
   \code{trans}, \code{cycle}, \code{four}, \code{triangle} \tab \code{ttriple}, \code{ctriple}, \code{cycle(4)}, \code{triangle} \cr
}


Read from ergm, \code{nodecov("x")} becomes \code{ego(x) + alter(x)}, and \code{edges}
and \code{density} are the intercept, which an MRQAP always has.
ergm's terms of the outcome's own structure, such as \code{gwesp()}, and its
node fixed effects, \code{sender}, \code{receiver}, and \code{sociality}, are kept for
ergm and dropped for an MRQAP, where \code{(1 | sender)} adds a random
intercept instead.
The left-hand side of an ergm formula names the network rather than a
layer, so where it names no layer or tie attribute of \code{.data}, the
outcome is read as \code{.}.

A formula written for ergm is returned as it is.
Written for ergm, every other term, such as \code{sim()}, \code{indeg()}, or an
interaction, is \code{edgecov()} of the matrix infernet builds, which needs
\code{.data}. With \code{.data}, the formula's environment holds those matrices,
and the outcome as a \code{network} object under its own name, so
\code{ergm::ergm(specify_ergm(f, .data))} fits as it is.
\code{edges} is always added, as ergm's intercept.
\code{(1 | sender)} and \code{(1 | receiver)} become ergm's fixed effects \code{sender}
and \code{receiver}, which are the nearest ergm has.
A cognitive social structure or a list of networks aborts, since ergm
fits one report of one network.
}

\section{goldfish and infernet}{

goldfish's terms are infernet's where the two packages share them.
\code{sim()} is 1 - |d| / range in infernet and -|d| in goldfish, and
\code{tertius()} and \code{tertius_diff()} leave out the sender in infernet and
include it in goldfish. Both are kept and named.

For an MRQAP, goldfish's endogenous effects, such as \code{inertia}, \code{trans},
\code{cycle}, \code{common_sender}, \code{common_receiver}, and \code{four}, read the
outcome's own ties, which permuting the outcome would not permute with
them, so they are dropped. So are goldfish's closures through two layers
and the DyNAM-i statistics of groups.

For goldfish, a layer named on its own becomes \code{tie(layer, weighted = TRUE)},
\code{exchange(layer)} becomes \code{recip(layer)}, and \code{dist(x)} becomes \code{diff(x)}.
\code{match()}, \code{mix()}, and random intercepts have no goldfish term, and are
dropped.
}

